// Specialised: claims, unrest and rebellion.

:: anchor=left size=20 pad=26 enter=type speed=44 line=1.4

@Signals Officer
:: point=tab.claims gate=1 until=open:claims
Claims and unrest. Open the claims panel. These are one subject, whatever the interface says.
---
@Signals Officer
A §**claim**§ is the difference between a war the world tolerates and a war
that unites everyone against you. Press one you hold and you are settling a
grievance; take ground you never claimed and you are the grievance.
---
@Signals Officer
Make the claim §**before**§ the war, not after. A claim filed the turn after
you took the province fools nobody and costs the same.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=panel.close gate=1 until=closed:claims
Close it.
---
@Signals Officer
§**Unrest**§ is what taking ground does to the ground. Conquered provinces
resent it, minorities resent it more, and conscription and censorship both
buy you strength now against unrest later.
---
@Signals Officer
Past a threshold a province stops paying and starts organising. Past the next
one it leaves -- and it leaves as a §**country**§, with your industry, your
population and a very specific opinion of you.

You get a warning. You do not always get a second one.
---
@Signals Officer
The cures are unpopular or slow: garrison it, spend on it, or give it what it
wants. Choose early, because all three take turns to work.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=tab.politics gate=1 until=open:politics
Spending on it is where most of it goes to waste. Open Politics, then
§**Districts**§.

---
@Signals Officer
The budget used to fall on the whole country at once -- the same weight on a
quiet county as on the one that is arming. So cut the country up. A
§**district**§ is a piece of it you govern as a unit: paint provinces into it,
give it a colour, give it a §**share**§. The shares always add to one hundred,
because there is only one budget.

---
@Signals Officer
What matters is the share of the §**money**§ against the share of the
§**ground**§. A district holding a tenth of the country on a tenth of the
budget is policed exactly as it was before -- so dividing changes nothing until
you make the shares uneven.

---
@Signals Officer
Draw a small district around the trouble, hand it a quarter of the money, and
it is policed several times harder; the panel tells you the figure. Everywhere
else pays for it, and that is the decision. There is a button to split evenly:
a sensible place to start and rarely the place to stay.

A district also passes its own §**regional law**§: a curfew, a tax
holiday, whose language the courts use. Small, local, and each one a trade.

---
@Signals Officer
:: point=panel.close gate=1 until=closed:politics
Close it.

---
@Signals Officer
That is unrest. Anything else?
